&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Speaker:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;Furong Huang, Associate Professor, Department of Computer Science at the University of Maryland&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Registration for all CUID holders is preferred. If you do not have an active CUID, registration is required and is due at 12:00 PM the day prior to the seminar. Unfortunately, we cannot guarantee entrance to Columbia&amp;rsquo;s Morningside campus if you register following 12:00 PM the day prior to the seminar. Thank you for understanding!&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://form.jotform.com/DSI_Events/ML-AI-Seminar-Series" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;REGISTER&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Title:&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;Rethinking Test-Time Thinking: From Token-Level Rewards to Robust Generative Agents&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Abstract:&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;We present a unified perspective on test-time thinking as a lens for improving generative AI agents through finer-grained reward modeling, data-centric reasoning, and robust alignment. Beginning with &lt;a href="https://urldefense.com/v3/__https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.08193__;!!BDUfV1Et5lrpZQ!WyBnTLjoVm3EsN2GzrJQNS0n-hnjs-OSlAKL6g028w2m6kyKqciMbRqMuTmWvT2g-JIh6RgJWLkiQY0fEkrLbw$"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;GenARM&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, we introduce an inductive bias for denser, token-level reward modeling that guides generation during decoding, enabling token-level alignment without retraining. While GenARM targets reward design, &lt;a href="https://urldefense.com/v3/__https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.07934__;!!BDUfV1Et5lrpZQ!WyBnTLjoVm3EsN2GzrJQNS0n-hnjs-OSlAKL6g028w2m6kyKqciMbRqMuTmWvT2g-JIh6RgJWLkiQY1NmeWQUA$"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;ThinkLite-VL&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; focuses on the data side of reasoning. It proposes a self-improvement framework that selects the most informative samples via MCTS-guided search, yielding stronger visual reasoning with fewer labels. Taking this a step further, &lt;a href="https://urldefense.com/v3/__https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.05523__;!!BDUfV1Et5lrpZQ!WyBnTLjoVm3EsN2GzrJQNS0n-hnjs-OSlAKL6g028w2m6kyKqciMbRqMuTmWvT2g-JIh6RgJWLkiQY2FnvZ7Bw$"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;MORSE-500&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; moves beyond selection to creation: it programmatically generates targeted, controllable multimodal data to systematically probe and stress-test models&amp;rsquo; reasoning abilities. We then interrogate a central assumption in inference-time alignment: &lt;a href="https://urldefense.com/v3/__https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.04210__;!!BDUfV1Et5lrpZQ!WyBnTLjoVm3EsN2GzrJQNS0n-hnjs-OSlAKL6g028w2m6kyKqciMbRqMuTmWvT2g-JIh6RgJWLkiQY3Gt89TJw$"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Does Thinking More Always Help?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; Our findings reveal that increased reasoning steps can degrade performance--not due to better or worse reasoning per se, but due to rising variance in outputs, challenging the naive scaling paradigm. Finally, &lt;a href="https://urldefense.com/v3/__https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20965__;!!BDUfV1Et5lrpZQ!WyBnTLjoVm3EsN2GzrJQNS0n-hnjs-OSlAKL6g028w2m6kyKqciMbRqMuTmWvT2g-JIh6RgJWLkiQY2z11_56g$"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;AegisLLM&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; applies test-time thinking in the service of security, using an agentic, multi-perspective framework to defend against jailbreaks, prompt injections, and unlearning attacks--all at inference time. Together, these works chart a path toward generative agents that are not only more capable, but more data-efficient, introspective, and robust in real-world deployment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Registration REQUIRED by 4pm on October 23, 2025 to attend this event.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Please join the Ukrainian Studies Program at the Harriman Institute for a poetry reading and discussion with &lt;strong&gt;Sasha Dugdale&lt;/strong&gt; and&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Oksana Maksymchuk&lt;/strong&gt;. Moderated by&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://harriman.columbia.edu/person/mark-andryczyk/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mark Andryczyk&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Contemporary poetry can challenge, explore uncomfortable truths, and bear witness to unimaginable experiences. In Sasha Dugdale&amp;rsquo;s new collection, &amp;quot;The Strongbox,&amp;quot; recent history and Greek mythology meet. Her varied cast of characters are abducted to foreign lands, travel through war zones, and are haunted by conflicts. Oksana Maksymchuk&amp;rsquo;s &amp;quot;Still City&amp;quot; reflects life in the wake of extreme and unpredictable violence. Beginning as a poetic journal kept from her hometime in Ukraine, 2021-22, it chronicles events as the poet, her family and community prepare for airstrikes, as well as nuclear, chemical and biological warfare. Together, Oksana and Sasha discuss what it is to write poetry in these times of conflict.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You&amp;rsquo;re invited to join Columbia University&amp;rsquo;s M.S. Nonprofit Management program for an informal professional development webinar with Program Director and Professor of Practice Dr. Basil A. Smikle Jr., in conversation with Rebecca L. Smith, LMSW, Director of Philanthropy for The Salvation Army of Greater New York.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;An alum of Columbia University&amp;rsquo;s M.S. Nonprofit Management Program, Rebecca began her professional career as a social worker and therapist in the underfunded specialty of severe mental illness and discovered her calling to raise funds for social services while attending her first gala.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;At The Salvation Army of Greater New York, Rebecca leads a large team responsible for all fundraising, marketing and public relations in downstate New York. She previously served as a consultant with CCS Fundraising, where she developed and implemented comprehensive fundraising plans and supported operational restructuring efforts across a wide variety of sectors.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For questions, please contact us at SPS-Nonprofit@columbia.edu.&lt;br /&gt;
